<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Psellos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1018–1078</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Michael Psellus, Psellos, Constantine Psellos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Psellos was a Byzantine monk, writer, philosopher, historian and imperial courtier who advised several 11th-century emperors. Born Constantine, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in 1017 or 1018, he rose from the imperial chancellery to advise Constantine IX Monomachos and to be the leading professor at the University of Constantinople, and he played a decisive part in the changes of ruler of 1057, 1059 and 1071. His Chronographia, biographies of 14 emperors and empresses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> to Michael VII Doukas, is a valuable source on the 11th-century empire, and his revival of classical studies later influenced the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Most scholars believe he died soon after 1078.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-psellos.jpg" alt="Michael Psellos" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown/Άγνωστος · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Psellos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Learning was the usual way in: <b>Michael Psellos</b> began as a secretary and became adviser to several emperors.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
